2. Mathematical framework

2.1 Observables and conditional extensionality

Definition 1 (Observable). A verifier is a map $f : \mathcal{P} \times \mathcal{L} \to \{\mathsf{V}, \mathsf{N}, \mathsf{U}\}$ realised as a pair $(O, \phi)$ where $O : \mathcal{P} \times \mathcal{L} \to \mathcal{O}$ takes values in a measurable observation space $(\mathcal{O}, \Sigma)$ and $\phi : \mathcal{O} \to \{\mathsf{V}, \mathsf{N}, \mathsf{U}\}$ is a decision rule, so that $f(P,L) = \phi(O(P,L))$.

Definition 2 (Conditional extensionality). Let $Z$ be side-information — runner identity, library version, wall-clock epoch, mirror snapshot, prior-trial cache state. The verifier $f = (O, \phi)$ is extensional given $Z$ if

O(P, L; Z) = O(P', L'; Z) \implies f(P, L; Z) = f(P', L'; Z).

Unconditional extensionality is the special case $Z \equiv \varnothing$. The weakening is substantive: a verifier with a cached decision from a prior trial, or one that queries a live registry, is not unconditionally extensional, but it may be conditionally extensional on the cache state or on the registry snapshot. The theory below is stated for the conditional version; the empirical protocol measures the extensionality-violation rate $\mathbb{P}(O(P,L;Z) = O(P',L';Z), \; f(P,L;Z) \neq f(P',L';Z))$ and treats it as a first-class quantity, reported alongside coverage and false-VERIFIABLE, not assumed to be zero.

Remark 2.1. Unconditional extensionality is recovered when the side-information carries no information about the decision, i.e., when $f$ is a deterministic function of $(P,L)$. Verifiers with session caches, time-dependent parsing order, or live network queries fall outside this special case. Restricting the theory to the unconditional case would silently exclude a large fraction of real format-level verifiers; the conditional formulation keeps them in scope at the cost of an extra measured quantity.

2.2 Tri-state decision theory with a fallible reference

Definition 3 (Measured soundness, completeness, coverage). On a loss set $\mathcal{L}_0 \subseteq \mathcal{L}$, with respect to the measured reference $R$:

· $f$ is $R$-sound on $\mathcal{L}_0$ if $\mathbb{P}\big(f = \mathsf{V} \mid R = \mathsf{N}, \mathcal{L}_0\big) = 0$.
· $f$ is $R$-complete on $\mathcal{L}_0$ if $\mathbb{P}\big(f = \mathsf{V} \mid R = \mathsf{V}, \mathcal{L}_0\big) = 1$.
· Coverage is $C(f) = \mathbb{P}(f \neq \mathsf{U})$.
· Measured false-VERIFIABLE rate is $\mathrm{FV}(f) = \mathbb{P}\big(f = \mathsf{V} \mid R = \mathsf{N}\big)$.

Definition 4 (True soundness and reference error). Let $R^\star(P,L) \in \{\mathsf{V}, \mathsf{N}\}$ be the true re-executability label, unobservable. The reference error on $\mathcal{L}_0$ is

\epsilon_{\text{ref}}(\mathcal{L}_0) = \mathbb{P}\big(R \neq R^\star \mid \mathcal{L}_0\big),

with class-conditional versions $\epsilon_{\mathsf{V}} = \mathbb{P}(R = \mathsf{N} \mid R^\star = \mathsf{V})$ and $\epsilon_{\mathsf{N}} = \mathbb{P}(R = \mathsf{V} \mid R^\star = \mathsf{N})$.

Proposition 1 (Propagation of reference error into soundness). For any verifier $f$ with measured false-VERIFIABLE rate $\mathrm{FV}(f)$ on $\mathcal{L}_0$, the true false-VERIFIABLE rate satisfies

\mathrm{FV}^\star(f) \le \frac{\mathrm{FV}(f) + \epsilon_{\mathsf{N}}}{1 - \epsilon_{\mathsf{V}} - \epsilon_{\mathsf{N}}},

and

\mathrm{FV}^\star(f) \ge \frac{\mathrm{FV}(f) - \epsilon_{\mathsf{V}}}{1 - \epsilon_{\mathsf{V}} - \epsilon_{\mathsf{N}}},

provided $\epsilon_{\mathsf{V}} + \epsilon_{\mathsf{N}} < 1$.

Proof. Write $\mathbb{P}(f = \mathsf{V}, R^\star = \mathsf{N})$ as $\mathbb{P}(f = \mathsf{V}, R = \mathsf{N}) \cdot \mathbb{P}(R^\star = \mathsf{N} \mid f = \mathsf{V}, R = \mathsf{N}) + \mathbb{P}(f = \mathsf{V}, R = \mathsf{V}) \cdot \mathbb{P}(R^\star = \mathsf{N} \mid f = \mathsf{V}, R = \mathsf{V})$. The first term is at most $\mathrm{FV}(f) \cdot \mathbb{P}(R = \mathsf{N})$; the second is at most $\epsilon_{\mathsf{V}}$. Applying Bayes to the denominator $1 - \epsilon_{\mathsf{V}} - \epsilon_{\mathsf{N}}$ and simplifying gives the upper bound; the lower bound follows symmetrically. $\square$

Corollary 1.1. If $\mathrm{FV}(f) \le \alpha$ and the reference is unbiased ($\epsilon_{\mathsf{N}} \le \epsilon_{\mathsf{V}}$), then $\mathrm{FV}^\star(f) \le \alpha + \epsilon_{\mathsf{N}}/(1 - \epsilon_{\mathsf{V}} - \epsilon_{\mathsf{N}})$. The reported bound is therefore $R$-sound but not $R^\star$-sound at level $\alpha$ unless $\epsilon_{\text{ref}}$ is measured and subtracted.

Remark 1.1. The earlier version of this work treated $R$ as ground truth and reported $\mathrm{FV}(f) \le 0.05$ as if it certified true false-VERIFIABLE. Proposition 1 shows that this certificate is conditional on $\epsilon_{\mathsf{ref}} = 0$, which the three-run protocol with $\bot$ exclusions cannot guarantee. The protocol in §10 therefore measures $\epsilon_{\mathsf{ref}}$ from the $\bot$ rate and the disagreement rate between paired protocols, reports it, and inflates the certified bound accordingly.

2.3 Coverage–risk frontier and cost-matrix sensitivity

Definition 5 (Frontier and envelope). For a family $\{f_\lambda\}_{\lambda \in \Lambda}$ parameterised by $\lambda$ (including the mapping threshold $\psi$), the coverage–risk frontier is

\mathcal{F} = \big\{ (C(f_\lambda), \mathrm{FV}(f_\lambda)) : \lambda \in \Lambda \big\} \subset [0,1]^2,

and its sound envelope is the lower-left monotone hull. Sufficiency is a region-intersection problem: is there $\lambda$ with $C(f_\lambda) \ge c_0$ and $\mathrm{FV}(f_\lambda) \le \alpha$?

Definition 6 (Cost-parameterised adoption). For cost triple $(c_{\mathsf{FV}}, c_{\mathsf{FN}}, c_{\mathsf{U}}) \in \mathbb{R}_{>0}^3$, the Bayes-optimal mapping $\psi^\ast_{c}$ is defined by Proposition 5 below. The adoption set is

\mathcal{A}(c) = \big\{ f : C(f_{\psi^\ast_{c}}) \ge c_0, \; \mathrm{FV}(f_{\psi^\ast_{c}}) \le \alpha, \; \mathrm{FV}_{\text{worst}}(f_{\psi^\ast_{c}}) \le \alpha_{\text{worst}} \big\}.

The adoption decision is cost-robust on a set $\mathcal{C}_0 \subset \mathbb{R}_{>0}^3$ if $\mathcal{A}(c)$ is constant on $\mathcal{C}_0$.

Proposition 2 (Cost-matrix flip points). Under the two-threshold rule of Corollary 4, the $\mathsf{N}$/$\mathsf{U}$/$\mathsf{V}$ regions partition $[0,1]$ iff $c_{\mathsf{U}} < c_{\mathsf{FV}} c_{\mathsf{FN}}/(c_{\mathsf{FV}} + c_{\mathsf{FN}})$. The regions are ordered $\mathsf{N} < \mathsf{U} < \mathsf{V}$ in $p$ precisely when the non-degeneracy condition holds; when it fails, the $\mathsf{U}$ region is empty and the rule is the binary Bayes classifier with threshold $c_{\mathsf{FV}}/(c_{\mathsf{FV}} + c_{\mathsf{FN}})$.

Proof. See §8.2, where the corrected thresholds are derived. $\square$

Remark 2.2. The earlier version pre-registered a single cost triple $(c_{\mathsf{FV}}, c_{\mathsf{FN}}, c_{\mathsf{U}}) = (10, 2, 1)$ and reported the adoption decision under that triple as if it were objective. Proposition 2 makes the sensitivity explicit: the $\mathsf{U}$ region exists only when abstention is cheaper than the effective error cost, and the adoption decision is a function of the triple. §10.4 reports the adoption decision on a grid of 27 cost triples and identifies the flip surface.

2.4 Separation cost and parsimony

Definition 7 (Evidence cost and parsimony). For $f = (O, \phi)$, let $k(f) = |O(f)|$ be the number of distinct evidence types the observable draws from. Define parsimony $\pi(f) = 1/(1 + k(f)) \in (0, 1]$.

Definition 8 (Separation cost). For a mixed-label loss set $\mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{P} \times \mathcal{L}$,

k^\star(\mathcal{S}) = \inf\big\{ k \in \mathbb{N} : \exists\, f \text{ with } k(f) \le k, \; f \text{ sound and complete on } \mathcal{S}, \; C(f \mid \mathcal{S}) > 0 \big\}.

The separation cost is the minimum number of evidence types required to discriminate $\mathsf{V}$ from $\mathsf{N}$ on $\mathcal{S}$ with soundness, completeness, and positive coverage.

