5. Class-specific separation costs and the trilemma

5.1 The trilemma, made non-definitional

The earlier version stated the trilemma as a consequence of the definition of $k^\star$. This is correct but tautological. The substantive version below proves class-specific lower bounds on $k^\star$, so that the trilemma becomes a statement about which evidence types are necessary for specific loss classes, not a conditional statement about an abstract quantity.

Proposition 5 (Class-specific separation costs). Consider three dependency-loss classes:

· $\mathcal{S}_{\text{src}}$: source URLs only (bytes intact, source removed).
· $\mathcal{S}_{\text{pkg}}$: package registry only (bytes and sources intact, package removed).
· $\mathcal{S}_{\text{data}}$: data URLs only (bytes and sources intact, data removed).

Under the assumption that (i) the required components are declared in $M$, and (ii) some packages are unaffected by a source loss and vice versa, the following lower bounds hold:

1. $k^\star(\mathcal{S}_{\text{src}}) \ge 2$: no single evidence type both certifies byte integrity and certifies source availability on a mixed set.
2. $k^\star(\mathcal{S}_{\text{pkg}}) \ge 2$: byte digest plus source resolution does not separate $\mathsf{V}$ from $\mathsf{N}$ on $\mathcal{S}_{\text{pkg}}$; a package-resolution evidence type is required.
3. $k^\star(\mathcal{S}_{\text{data}}) \ge 2$: analogous to $\mathcal{S}_{\text{pkg}}$, with a data-availability evidence type.
4. $k^\star(\mathcal{S}_{\text{mixed}}) \ge 3$ for the union of the three classes with non-degenerate reference mass: no two of $\{$byte digest, source resolution, package resolution, data availability$\}$ separate all three loss classes simultaneously.

Proof. (1) Suppose $k(f) = 1$ with $O = O_{\text{byte}}$. By Corollary 4, $f$ is unsound on $\mathcal{S}_{\text{src}}$ if it maps match to $\mathsf{V}$. If it maps match to $\mathsf{N}$, it is incomplete on the intact trials in $\mathcal{S}_{\text{src}}$ where $R = \mathsf{V}$. If it abstains, coverage is zero. Hence no single-evidence observable works. (2) Suppose $k(f) = 2$ with $O = (O_{\text{byte}}, O_{\text{src}})$. A package removal leaves $B$ and the source intact but removes the package from the mirror; the pair $(O_{\text{byte}}, O_{\text{src}})$ does not detect the removal. By Proposition 4 applied to the subpopulation where $(O_{\text{byte}}, O_{\text{src}})$ is constant, $f$ is not sound, not complete, or abstains. (3) Analogous. (4) The union has three distinct failure modes; each requires a distinct evidence type to detect, and by (1)–(3) no two suffice for all three. $\square$

Remark 5.1. The lower bounds are conditional on the assumption that some templates are affected by one loss but not others, i.e., that the loss classes are not perfectly correlated. If every package that has a source URL also has a registry entry, and the two are removed together, the bound collapses to 2. The empirical protocol measures the loss-class correlation and reports the class-specific $k^\star$ under the measured correlation, not the idealised one.

5.2 The trilemma in non-definitional form

Proposition 6 (Memory-legibility trilemma). For any $\mathcal{S}$ on which $R$ takes both labels, the following hold:

1. Every verifier that discriminates $\mathsf{V}$ from $\mathsf{N}$ on $\mathcal{S}$ in the sense of Definition 8 satisfies $k(f) \ge k^\star(\mathcal{S})$, hence $\pi(f) \le 1/(1 + k^\star(\mathcal{S}))$.
2. If $k^\star(\mathcal{S}) = \infty$ — e.g., on a class where required components are not declared in $M$, so that no observable of $M$ can reveal their absence — no verifier attains $C = 1$ and $\sigma = 1$ simultaneously; it must accept $\mathrm{FV} > 0$, abstain on a positive-measure set, or both.

Proof. (1) is the definitional direction; it is stated for completeness and is not the substantive content. (2) follows by Proposition 4 applied to the subpopulation where the observable is constant: if the required components are undeclared, the manifest observable is constant across availability states that change $R$, so any verifier is unsound, incomplete, or abstains on a positive-measure set. $\square$

Remark 5.2. The substantive content of the trilemma is the class-specific lower bound of Proposition 5, not the definitional statement of Proposition 6. The latter is included for completeness; the former is what gives the trilemma empirical teeth.

5.3 Worst-class vs pooled

Lemma 2 (Pooled vs worst-class). Let $\{C_1, \dots, C_K\}$ be the dependency-loss classes with weights $w_c = \mathbb{P}(C_c \mid R = \mathsf{N})$ summing to $1$. Then

\mathrm{FV}_{\text{pooled}} = \sum_c w_c \, \mathrm{FV}_c, \qquad \mathrm{FV}_{\text{worst}} = \max_c \mathrm{FV}_c.

The pooled bound $\mathrm{FV}_{\text{pooled}} \le \alpha$ does not imply $\mathrm{FV}_{\text{worst}} \le 2\alpha$ unless $w_c \ge 1/2$ for the worst class.

Proof. If $w_c < 1/2$ and $\mathrm{FV}_c = \alpha/w_c$, then $\mathrm{FV}_{\text{pooled}} = \alpha$ while $\mathrm{FV}_{\text{worst}} = \alpha/w_c > 2\alpha$. $\square$

Consequence. The co-primary worst-class bound of $10\%$ is genuinely needed; with $K = 6$ equal-weight classes, $\mathrm{FV}_{\text{pooled}} \le 5\%$ permits $\mathrm{FV}_{\text{worst}} \le 30\%$. The worst-class test requires a multiplicity correction, treated in §9.5.

