7. Ablation calculus

7.1 Operations

Definition 12 (Ablation). For each class $c \in \{\text{source}, \text{data}, \text{perturbation}, \text{package}, \text{environment}, \text{third-party}\}$, an ablation operator is a map $A_c : \mathcal{P} \times \mathcal{L} \to \mathcal{P} \times \mathcal{L}$ such that $A_c(P,L) = (P, L')$ with $L' \subseteq L$ for loss classes, and $A_{\text{pert}}(P,L) = (P', L)$ with $M' \neq M$ for the perturbation class.

Remark 9.1 (Loss ≠ perturbation). For loss classes, $B$ and $M$ are unchanged; only availability shrinks. For perturbation, $M$ changes; availability is unchanged. Conflating the two corrupts the estimand, because a verifier that parses $M$ detects perturbations but not losses.

7.2 Lattice structure

Proposition 9. For loss classes $c, c'$, the operators $A_c \circ A_{c'}$ and $A_{c'} \circ A_c$ agree on the loss state, are idempotent, and are monotone: $A_c \circ A_c = A_c$. For perturbation, $A_{\text{pert}}$ is not idempotent in general and does not commute with loss operators.

Proof. Immediate from the definitions. $\square$

Remark 9.2. The loss states form a distributive lattice under $\subseteq$; the perturbation operator acts on the manifest only, not on the lattice. The empirical protocol reports the loss-class results on the lattice and the perturbation results separately.

