8. Tri-state mapping and the cost-matrix frontier

8.1 Bayes-optimal mapping

Proposition 10 (Bayes-optimal abstention, corrected). Fix a trial $(P,L)$ and let $p = \mathbb{P}(R = \mathsf{V} \mid O(P,L))$ be the posterior probability that the measured reference is $\mathsf{V}$. Fix costs $c_{\mathsf{FV}}, c_{\mathsf{FN}}, c_{\mathsf{U}} > 0$. The Bayes-optimal rule is

\psi^\ast(o) = \begin{cases} \mathsf{V} & \text{if } (1-p)c_{\mathsf{FV}} \le \min\{p c_{\mathsf{FN}}, c_{\mathsf{U}}\}, \\ \mathsf{N} & \text{if } p c_{\mathsf{FN}} \le \min\{(1-p)c_{\mathsf{FV}}, c_{\mathsf{U}}\}, \\ \mathsf{U} & \text{otherwise}. \end{cases}

Proof. The expected costs of $\mathsf{V}, \mathsf{N}, \mathsf{U}$ are $(1-p)c_{\mathsf{FV}}$, $p c_{\mathsf{FN}}$, $c_{\mathsf{U}}$. The Bayes action minimises. $\square$

Corollary 10 (Two-threshold form, corrected). Under the non-degeneracy condition $c_{\mathsf{U}} < c_{\mathsf{FV}} c_{\mathsf{FN}}/(c_{\mathsf{FV}} + c_{\mathsf{FN}})$, the Bayes rule is

\psi^\ast(o) = \begin{cases} \mathsf{N} & \text{if } p \le \tau_L, \\ \mathsf{U} & \text{if } \tau_L < p < \tau_H, \\ \mathsf{V} & \text{if } p \ge \tau_H, \end{cases}

with $\tau_L = c_{\mathsf{U}}/c_{\mathsf{FN}}$ and $\tau_H = 1 - c_{\mathsf{U}}/c_{\mathsf{FV}}$. If the non-degeneracy fails, the $\mathsf{U}$ region is empty and the rule is the binary Bayes classifier with threshold $c_{\mathsf{FV}}/(c_{\mathsf{FV}} + c_{\mathsf{FN}})$.

Proof. Direct enumeration of the three optimality regions. $\square$

Remark 10.1 (Correction). The earlier version stated $\tau_L = c_{\mathsf{U}}/(c_{\mathsf{U}} + c_{\mathsf{FN}})$; the derivation above gives $\tau_L = c_{\mathsf{U}}/c_{\mathsf{FN}}$. The corrected threshold enlarges the $\mathsf{N}$ region and shrinks the $\mathsf{U}$ region at the lower end. The correction is material for the protocol (§10.4) and is used throughout the revised version.

8.2 Cost-matrix frontier

Definition 13 (Cost frontier). For a grid $\mathcal{G} \subset \mathbb{R}_{>0}^3$ of cost triples, the cost frontier is the set

\mathcal{F}_{\text{cost}} = \big\{ \psi^\ast_c : c \in \mathcal{G} \big\}.

For each $c$, the adoption decision $D(c) \in \{\text{ADOPT}(f), \text{PILOT}, \text{FAIL}, \text{INCONCLUSIVE}\}$ is computed. The flip surface is the locus of cost triples at which $D(c)$ changes.

Proposition 11 (Monotone flip structure). Along any ray in cost space that fixes the ratios $c_{\mathsf{FV}}/c_{\mathsf{FN}}$ and $c_{\mathsf{U}}/c_{\mathsf{FN}}$ and scales the overall magnitude, the adoption decision is constant. Along a ray that fixes $c_{\mathsf{FV}}/c_{\mathsf{FN}}$ and varies $c_{\mathsf{U}}/c_{\mathsf{FN}}$, the adoption decision changes at most once, at the threshold at which the $\mathsf{U}$ region collapses.

Proof. The mapping $\psi^\ast$ depends only on the ratios, not the absolute magnitudes. Along the $\mathsf{U}$-ratio ray, the $\mathsf{U}$ region is non-empty iff the non-degeneracy condition holds, which is a single threshold inequality. $\square$

Remark 10.2. The protocol reports the adoption decision on a $3 \times 3 \times 3$ grid of cost triples with $c_{\mathsf{FV}} \in \{2, 10, 100\}$, $c_{\mathsf{FN}} \in \{1, 2, 10\}$, $c_{\mathsf{U}} \in \{0.1, 1, 2\}$ (total 27 triples). If the decision is constant across the grid, it is declared cost-robust. If not, the flip surface is reported, and the adoption decision is conditioned on the triple. The earlier version's single-triple decision is a special case of this frontier.

