9. Statistical theory

9.1 Beta-binomial model

Model. Trials are grouped into $T$ templates, each with $m$ variants. Let $X_{ij} \in \{0,1\}$ indicate false-VERIFIABLE at trial $j$ of template $i$. Then $p_i \sim \mathrm{Beta}(\alpha, \beta)$ and $X_{ij} \mid p_i \sim \mathrm{Bernoulli}(p_i)$.

Lemma 3 (Beta-binomial moments). Under the model,

\mu = \mathbb{E}[X_{ij}] = \frac{\alpha}{\alpha + \beta}, \qquad \rho = \mathrm{Corr}(X_{ij}, X_{ik}) = \frac{1}{\alpha + \beta + 1} \quad (j \neq k).

Proof. Law of total variance and standard beta moments. $\square$

9.2 Effective sample size

Lemma 4 (Design effect). With $\bar X = (1/(Tm)) \sum_{ij} X_{ij}$,

\mathrm{Var}(\bar X) = \frac{\mu(1-\mu)}{Tm}[1 + (m-1)\rho], \qquad n_{\text{eff}}(\rho; T, m) = \frac{Tm}{1 + (m-1)\rho}.

Proof. Covariance decomposition within templates; independence across templates. $\square$

9.3 Exact zero-error bounds

Lemma 5 (Clopper-Pearson zero-error upper bound). With $X = 0$ in $n$ trials, the one-sided $95\%$ CP upper bound on $p$ is $U = 1 - 0.05^{1/n}$.

Proof. The CP upper bound is the $0.95$-quantile of $\mathrm{Beta}(X+1, n-X)$; at $X=0$ this is $\mathrm{Beta}(1,n)$, whose survival function is $(1-p)^n$. Set $(1-U)^n = 0.05$. $\square$

Lemma 6 (Zero-abstention lower bound). With $n$ trials and zero abstentions, the one-sided $95\%$ CP lower bound on coverage is $L = 0.05^{1/(n+1)}$.

Proof. Symmetric to Lemma 5. $\square$

9.4 Design requirement

Proposition 12 (Design requirement for $H_1$). Under zero observed errors and plug-in $\rho_{\text{upper}}$, the required template count is

T_{\text{req}}(\rho; m) = \left\lceil \frac{n_0 [1 + (m-1)\rho]}{m} \right\rceil, \qquad n_0 = \left\lceil \frac{\ln 0.05}{\ln 0.95} \right\rceil = 59.

Proof. Set $1 - 0.05^{1/n} \le 0.05$ to get $n \ge \ln 0.05/\ln 0.95 \approx 58.4$, so $n_0 = 59$; substitute $n_{\text{eff}} = Tm/[1 + (m-1)\rho] \ge n_0$ and solve for $T$. $\square$

9.5 Multiplicity over loss classes

The earlier version treated the worst-class test as if the worst class were pre-specified. This is optimistically biased. The corrected protocol uses a max-$t$ correction over the $K$ loss classes.

Definition 14 (Max-$t$ worst-class bound). For each class $c \in \{1, \dots, K\}$, let $U_c$ be the one-sided $1-\alpha$ CP upper bound on the class-level false-VERIFIABLE rate. The max-$t$ worst-class bound is $\max_c U_c$, with $\alpha$ chosen so that the family-wise coverage over classes is at least $1 - \alpha_{\text{FWER}}$. Under the bootstrap approximation, $\alpha$ is obtained by resampling templates, computing $\max_c (U_c^\ast - U_c)/\hat\sigma_c$, and taking the $(1-\alpha_{\text{FWER}})$-quantile of the studentised maximum.

Proposition 13 (Family-wise validity of max-$t$). Under the bootstrap consistency conditions of Proposition 14, the max-$t$ interval $\max_c (U_c - q_{1-\alpha} \hat\sigma_c)$ covers $\max_c \mathrm{FV}_c$ with probability at least $1 - \alpha_{\text{FWER}} + o(1)$.

Proof. Standard step-down max-$t$ argument; bootstrap consistency of the studentised maximum follows from the Donsker condition and the Hadamard differentiability of the max functional. $\square$

9.6 Sample-splitting for $\rho$

The earlier version used a single development-set estimate $\rho_{\text{upper}}$ for both the design and the certification. This is optimistic: the same data are used to select the design and to certify the result.

Proposition 14 (Split-$\rho$ certification). Split the development set into two halves $\mathcal{D}_1, \mathcal{D}_2$. Estimate $\hat\rho_1$ on $\mathcal{D}_1$ and certify on $\mathcal{D}_2$ using $n_{\text{eff}}(\hat\rho_1)$, then swap and average. Under the beta-binomial model with $\hat\rho$ consistent for $\rho$, the split-$\rho$ certificate has coverage at least $1 - \alpha - o(1)$, without requiring $\hat\rho \ge \rho$ with probability one.

Proof. The split decouples the estimation error from the certification sample; the average of the two half-certificates is a V-fold cross-fit estimator, whose coverage follows from the standard cross-fitting argument. $\square$

Remark 12.1. The split-$\rho$ certificate is conservative relative to the oracle-$\rho$ certificate but not relative to the single-$\rho$ certificate of the earlier version, which was anti-conservative whenever the development and certification samples overlapped in $\rho$-estimation. The revised protocol uses split-$\rho$ and reports both the split-$\rho$ and single-$\rho$ bounds, with the split-$\rho$ bound as the certified one.

9.7 Bootstrap consistency

Proposition 15 (Cluster bootstrap consistency). Let $\hat p_b$ for $b = 1, \dots, B$ be the empirical false-VERIFIABLE rate under $T$ draws with replacement from the template population. Under (i) independence of templates, (ii) $\mathbb{E}[\hat p^2] < \infty$, and (iii) $\sqrt{T}(\hat p - \mu) \to \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2)$, the percentile cluster bootstrap upper bound $\hat U$ is consistent for $\mu + z_{0.95}\sigma/\sqrt{T}$.

Proof. Standard Donsker and quantile-map continuity argument; the empirical measure converges weakly, the bootstrap measure converges to the same Gaussian limit in probability, and the quantile map is Hadamard-differentiable. $\square$

Remark 12.2. At all-zero data, the percentile bootstrap returns $\hat U = 0$, which is anti-conservative. The implementation falls back to Lemma 5 with $n_{\text{eff}}$, pre-registered.

9.8 Nonzero $\mu_{\text{null}}$: exact computation

For $\mu_{\text{null}} > 0$, the certification probability $\mathbb{P}(U \le 0.05 \mid \mu = \mu_{\text{null}})$ is computed by exact binomial-tail enumeration:

\mathbb{P}(U \le 0.05 \mid \mu = \mu_{\text{null}}) = \sum_{k=0}^n \mathbb{P}(\mathrm{Bin}(n, \mu_{\text{null}}) = k) \, \mathbf{1}\{\mathbb{P}(\mathrm{Bin}(n, 0.05) \le k) \le 0.05\}.

This is exact and computable; the normal approximation is inaccurate at small $n$ and $\mu_{\text{null}}$ near zero. The design table is computed by exact enumeration.

