Appendix A — Conditional insensitivity and coverage ceiling

A.1 Proof of Proposition 4 (Conditional insensitivity)

Proposition 4. Let $f = (O, \phi)$ be extensional given side-information $Z$. Let $\mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{P} \times \mathcal{L}$ be a set of trials on which $O$ is constant given $Z$: $O(P,L;Z) = o^\star(Z)$ for all $(P,L) \in \mathcal{S}$. If there exist $(P,L), (P',L') \in \mathcal{S}$ with $R(P,L) = \mathsf{V}$ and $R(P',L') = \mathsf{N}$, then on $\mathcal{S}$:

1. if $\phi(o^\star(Z)) = \mathsf{V}$, $f$ is not $R$-sound;
2. if $\phi(o^\star(Z)) = \mathsf{N}$, $f$ is not $R$-complete;
3. if $\phi(o^\star(Z)) = \mathsf{U}$, $C(f \mid \mathcal{S}) = 0$.

Proof. Fix $Z$ (and if $Z$ varies within $\mathcal{S}$, condition on the stratum where $Z$ is constant; the argument applies stratum-wise). Conditional extensionality gives $\phi(O(P,L;Z)) = \phi(o^\star(Z))$ for all $(P,L)$ in the stratum, so $f$ outputs a single value on the stratum. The three cases follow:

Case (1): $f(P',L') = \mathsf{V}$ while $R(P',L') = \mathsf{N}$, so $f$ is not $R$-sound on $\mathcal{S}$.

Case (2): $f(P,L) = \mathsf{N}$ while $R(P,L) = \mathsf{V}$, so $f$ is not $R$-complete on $\mathcal{S}$.

Case (3): $f(P,L) = \mathsf{U}$ for all trials in the stratum, so $C(f \mid \mathcal{S}) = \mathbb{P}(f \neq \mathsf{U} \mid \mathcal{S}) = 0$. $\square$

Remark A.1. When $Z$ varies within $\mathcal{S}$ and $O$ is constant within each $Z$-stratum, the proposition applies separately to each stratum. The empirical protocol reports the insensitivity rate $\mathbb{P}(\text{stratum-constant} \mid \mathcal{S})$ and the extensionality-violation rate $\mathbb{P}(O(P,L;Z)=O(P',L';Z), \; f(P,L;Z) \neq f(P',L';Z) \mid \mathcal{S})$ as separate quantities. The proposition is a statement about the stratum-constant subset; the non-stratum-constant subset is an empirical object.

A.2 Proof of Corollary 4 (Integrity ceiling, conditional form)

Corollary 4. Let $O_{\mathrm{byte}} : \mathcal{P} \times \mathcal{L} \to \Sigma^\ast$ be the byte-digest observable, invariant across all loss states that preserve $B$. Any verifier $f = (O_{\mathrm{byte}}, \phi)$ that is conditionally extensional given the byte digest, and that maps a manifest match to $\mathsf{V}$, satisfies $\mathrm{FV}(f) = 1$ on the answered trials of any dependency-loss class with positive reference-$\mathsf{N}$ mass.

Proof. Because $O_{\mathrm{byte}}$ is a function of $B$ alone, it does not depend on $Z$; conditional extensionality given $Z$ is satisfied trivially for any $Z$ (the observation is $Z$-invariant). For $L \in \mathcal{L}$ a dependency-loss state with $B$ intact and $L \subsetneq C(P)$, we have $O_{\mathrm{byte}}(P,L) = O_{\mathrm{byte}}(P,C(P))$. By extensionality, $f(P,L) = \phi(O_{\mathrm{byte}}(P,L)) = \phi(O_{\mathrm{byte}}(P,C(P))) = f(P,C(P))$. Since $\phi$ maps a match to $\mathsf{V}$ and the intact trial has $R = \mathsf{V}$, $f(P,C(P)) = \mathsf{V}$, hence $f(P,L) = \mathsf{V}$.

Let $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{dep}} \subseteq \mathcal{L}$ be a dependency-loss class with positive reference-$\mathsf{N}$ mass (i.e., $\mathbb{P}(R = \mathsf{N} \mid \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{dep}}) > 0$). On every trial of $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{dep}}$ where $f$ answers, $f = \mathsf{V}$. Hence
\mathrm{FV}(f \mid \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{dep}}) = \frac{\mathbb{P}(f = \mathsf{V}, R = \mathsf{N} \mid \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{dep}})}{\mathbb{P}(R = \mathsf{N} \mid \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{dep}})} = 1. \qquad \square

Remark A.2. Corollary 4 is stated for the breaking dependency-loss subpopulation. If some dependency losses are non-breaking (the package is genuinely unused), the reference label on those trials is $\mathsf{V}$ by observed re-execution, and $f = \mathsf{V}$ is correct. The empirical protocol measures the reference label rather than assuming it.

A.3 Proof of Corollary 5 (Coverage ceiling)

Definition A.1 (Separation). $O$ separates $R$ at $(P,L) \in \mathcal{S}$ if there is no $(P',L') \in \mathcal{S}$ with $O(P,L;Z) = O(P',L';Z)$ and $R(P,L) \neq R(P',L')$. Write $\mathrm{Sep}(O,R;\mathcal{S})$ for the set of trials at which $O$ separates $R$.

Corollary 5. For any $R$-sound verifier $f = (O,\phi)$ and any mixed-label set $\mathcal{S}$,
C(f \mid \mathcal{S}) \le \mathbb{P}\big(\mathrm{Sep}(O,R;\mathcal{S})\big).

Proof. Suppose $(P,L) \in \mathcal{S} \setminus \mathrm{Sep}(O,R;\mathcal{S})$. Then there exists $(P',L') \in \mathcal{S}$ with $O(P,L;Z) = O(P',L';Z)$ and $R(P,L) \neq R(P',L')$. WLOG $R(P,L) = \mathsf{V}$, so $R(P',L') = \mathsf{N}$. By conditional extensionality given $Z$, $f(P,L;Z) = f(P',L';Z)$. Since $f$ is $R$-sound, $f(P',L';Z) \neq \mathsf{V}$; hence $f(P,L;Z) \neq \mathsf{V}$. If $f(P,L;Z) = \mathsf{N}$, then $f$ is $R$-incomplete at $(P,L)$; if $f(P,L;Z) = \mathsf{U}$, then $f$ abstains at $(P,L)$.

Thus on every trial in $\mathcal{S} \setminus \mathrm{Sep}(O,R;\mathcal{S})$ where $R = \mathsf{V}$, the verifier either abstains or is incomplete. Restricting to trials where $f$ does not abstain and applying the indicator decomposition,
C(f \mid \mathcal{S}) = \mathbb{P}(f \neq \mathsf{U} \mid \mathcal{S}) \le \mathbb{P}(\mathrm{Sep}(O,R;\mathcal{S})). \qquad \square

Remark A.3. Corollary 5 is stated relative to the measured reference $R$, not the true label $R^\star$. The propagation to $R^\star$ is via Proposition 1 (Appendix B), which inflates the bound by $\epsilon_{\mathrm{ref}}$.

