Appendix C — Class-specific separation costs

C.1 Proof of Proposition 5

Proposition 5. Consider three dependency-loss classes:

· $\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{src}}$: source URLs only (bytes intact, source removed).
· $\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{pkg}}$: package registry only (bytes and sources intact, package removed).
· $\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{data}}$: data URLs only (bytes and sources intact, data removed).

Under the assumptions (i) required components are declared in $M$, and (ii) some templates are affected by one loss but not the others (non-perfect correlation of loss classes), the following lower bounds hold:

1. $k^\star(\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{src}}) \ge 2$;
2. $k^\star(\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{pkg}}) \ge 2$;
3. $k^\star(\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{data}}) \ge 2$;
4. $k^\star(\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{mixed}}) \ge 3$ for the union with non-degenerate reference mass.

Proof.

(1) Suppose $k(f) = 1$ with $O = O_{\mathrm{byte}}$. By Corollary 4, $f$ is $R$-unsound on the breaking dependency-loss subpopulation if it maps match to $\mathsf{V}$; if it maps match to $\mathsf{N}$, it is $R$-incomplete on the intact trials where $R = \mathsf{V}$; if it abstains, $C = 0$. Hence no single-evidence observable is discriminating.

(2) Suppose $k(f) = 1$ with $O = O_{\mathrm{src}}$ (source resolution only). On $\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{src}}$, $O_{\mathrm{src}}$ detects the source removal; but on $\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{src}}$, some templates have intact sources and others do not, so $O_{\mathrm{src}}$ takes both values. However, on the subpopulation where the source is intact but $B$ has been corrupted (a different loss class), $O_{\mathrm{src}}$ cannot detect the corruption. More directly: on $\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{pkg}}$, both $B$ and the source are intact, so $O_{\mathrm{src}}$ is constant across the loss and the intact trial, and Proposition 4 applies. Hence a single source-resolution observable does not discriminate on $\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{pkg}}$. The argument for $\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{data}}$ is analogous.

(3) Analogous to (2).

(4) For the union $\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{mixed}} = \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{src}} \cup \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{pkg}} \cup \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{data}}$, we need an observable that detects source removal, package removal, and data removal. The available evidence types are byte digest, source resolution, package resolution, data availability. Any two of these cover at most two of the three failure modes:

· $\{$byte, source$\}$ misses package and data removals.
· $\{$byte, package$\}$ misses source and data removals.
· $\{$byte, data$\}$ misses source and package removals.
· $\{$source, package$\}$ misses data removals and does not verify byte integrity.
· $\{$source, data$\}$ misses package removals and does not verify byte integrity.
· $\{$package, data$\}$ misses source removals and does not verify byte integrity.

Each pair leaves at least one failure mode undetected, so by Proposition 4 applied to the undetected-mode subpopulation, no pair is discriminating on the union. Hence $k^\star \ge 3$. $\square$

Remark C.1. The exact value of $k^\star(\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{mixed}})$ depends on whether byte re-verification is required on loss trials. If it is, $k^\star = 4$ (byte + source + package + data); if not (the loss trials are assumed to have intact bytes by construction), $k^\star = 3$ (source + package + data). The lower bound $k^\star \ge 3$ holds in both cases. The protocol reports the value under both assumptions.

Remark C.2. The lower bound is conditional on assumption (ii). If every template affected by a source loss is also affected by a package loss, the bound collapses to 2. The protocol measures the loss-class correlation and reports $k^\star$ under the measured correlation.

C.2 Proof of Proposition 6 (Trilemma)

Proposition 6. For any $\mathcal{S}$ on which $R$ takes both labels:

1. Every verifier that discriminates $\mathsf{V}$ from $\mathsf{N}$ on $\mathcal{S}$ (sound, complete, positive coverage) satisfies $k(f) \ge k^\star(\mathcal{S})$, hence $\pi(f) \le 1/(1 + k^\star(\mathcal{S}))$.
2. If $k^\star(\mathcal{S}) = \infty$ — e.g., on a class where required components are not declared in $M$ — no verifier attains $C = 1$ and $\sigma = 1$ simultaneously.

Proof. (1) By definition of $k^\star$ as the infimum over sound, complete, positive-coverage verifiers, any such verifier has $k(f) \ge k^\star$. The parsimony bound follows from $\pi(f) = 1/(1 + k(f))$. (2) If the required components are undeclared, the manifest observable is constant across availability states that change $R$, and Proposition 4 applies: any verifier is unsound, incomplete, or abstains on a positive-measure set. Hence $(\sigma, C) \neq (1,1)$. $\square$

Remark C.3. The substantive content is the class-specific lower bound of Proposition 5, not the definitional statement of Proposition 6(1). The former gives the trilemma empirical teeth; the latter is included for completeness.

C.3 Proof of Lemma 2 (Pooled vs worst-class)

Lemma 2. Let $\{C_1, \dots, C_K\}$ be the dependency-loss classes with weights $w_c = \mathbb{P}(C_c \mid R = \mathsf{N})$ summing to $1$. Then
\mathrm{FV}_{\mathrm{pooled}} = \sum_c w_c \, \mathrm{FV}_c, \qquad \mathrm{FV}_{\mathrm{worst}} = \max_c \mathrm{FV}_c.


The pooled bound $\mathrm{FV}_{\mathrm{pooled}} \le \alpha$ does not imply $\mathrm{FV}_{\mathrm{worst}} \le 2\alpha$ unless $w_c \ge 1/2$ for the worst class.

Proof. If $w_c < 1/2$ and $\mathrm{FV}_c = \alpha/w_c$, then $\mathrm{FV}_{\mathrm{pooled}} = w_c \cdot (\alpha/w_c) = \alpha$, while $\mathrm{FV}_{\mathrm{worst}} = \alpha/w_c > 2\alpha$. $\square$

Consequence. With $K = 6$ equal-weight classes, $\mathrm{FV}_{\mathrm{pooled}} \le 5\%$ permits $\mathrm{FV}_{\mathrm{worst}} \le 30\%$. The co-primary worst-class bound of $10\%$ is genuinely needed.

