Appendix D — Correlated decay and the frailty model

D.1 Proof of Proposition 7 (Existence and representation)

Proposition 7. If $S : [0,\infty) \to [0,1]$ is right-continuous with $S(0) = 1$ and non-increasing, then $t_{1/2} = \inf\{t : S(t) \le 1/2\}$ and $t_\varepsilon = \inf\{t : S(t) \le \varepsilon\}$ exist in $[0,\infty]$ and are unique minima. If $S$ is absolutely continuous with hazard $\lambda(t) = -S'(t)/S(t)$, then $S(t) = \exp(-\int_0^t \lambda(s) ds)$ and $t_{1/2} = \Lambda^{-1}(\log 2)$ with $\Lambda(t) = \int_0^t \lambda(s) ds$.

Proof. Existence and uniqueness of the infimum follow from monotonicity and completeness of $\mathbb{R}$. The survival representation follows by solving $\lambda = -S'/S$ as a linear ODE with $S(0) = 1$. Absolute continuity gives $\Lambda$ continuous and non-decreasing; $\Lambda(t) = \log 2$ has a unique solution when $\Lambda(\infty) > \log 2$. $\square$

D.2 Proof of Proposition 8 (Frailty survival)

Definition D.1 (Shared frailty). For each package $P$, let $W_P \sim \mathrm{Gamma}(1/\theta, 1/\theta)$ with mean 1 and variance $\theta$. Conditional on $W_P = w$, component lifetimes are independent with survival $\bar F_c(t \mid w) = \bar F_{c,0}(t)^w$. Unconditionally, lifetimes are positively correlated with Kendall's $\tau = \theta/(\theta+2)$.

Proposition 8. Under Definition D.1 and the assumption that re-execution is possible iff every component in a required subset $C^\ast(P)$ survives,
S(t) = \mathbb{E}_P\!\left[\left(1 + \theta \sum_{c \in C^\ast(P)} \Lambda_{c,0}(t)\right)^{-1/\theta}\right],


where $\Lambda_{c,0}(t) = -\log \bar F_{c,0}(t)$.

Proof. Conditional on $W_P = w$ and $P$, the required subset survives with probability
\prod_{c \in C^\ast(P)} \bar F_{c,0}(t)^w = \exp\!\left(-w \sum_{c \in C^\ast(P)} \Lambda_{c,0}(t)\right).


Averaging over $W_P \sim \mathrm{Gamma}(1/\theta, 1/\theta)$ uses the Laplace transform $\mathbb{E}[e^{-w\Lambda}] = (1+\theta\Lambda)^{-1/\theta}$ with $\Lambda = \sum_c \Lambda_{c,0}(t)$. Then average over $P$. $\square$

D.3 Corollaries and corrected comparison

Corollary 8.1 (Independence limit). As $\theta \to 0$, $S(t) \to \mathbb{E}_P[\exp(-\sum_c \Lambda_{c,0}(t))] = \mathbb{E}_P[\prod_c \bar F_{c,0}(t)]$, recovering the independent-lifetime formula.

Proof. Standard limit $(1+\theta\Lambda)^{-1/\theta} \to e^{-\Lambda}$ as $\theta \to 0$. $\square$

Corollary 8.2 (Corrected comparison). For $\theta > 0$ and any $\Lambda > 0$:
(1+\theta\Lambda)^{-1/\theta} > e^{-\Lambda}.


Moreover, for any collection $\Lambda_1, \dots, \Lambda_d > 0$:
(1+\theta\sum_i \Lambda_i)^{-1/\theta} \ge \prod_i (1+\theta\Lambda_i)^{-1/\theta} \ge \prod_i e^{-\Lambda_i}.


Hence the frailty model predicts survival at least as large as the independent model with the same baseline hazards at every $t$, and its half-life is at least as long.

Proof. For the first inequality, take logarithms: $-\log(1+\theta\Lambda)/\theta > -\Lambda \iff \log(1+\theta\Lambda) < \theta\Lambda$, which holds for all $\theta\Lambda > 0$ by the standard inequality $\log(1+x) < x$. For the second, the product inequality $\prod_i (1+\theta\Lambda_i) \ge 1+\theta\sum_i \Lambda_i$ follows by induction on $d$ (base case $d=1$ is trivial; induction step uses $(1+\theta\Lambda_{d})(1+S) = 1+\theta\Lambda_d + S + \theta\Lambda_d S \ge 1+\theta(\Lambda_d + S)$). Raising both sides to the power $-1/\theta$ (a decreasing map) reverses the inequality. The third inequality applies the first factor-wise. $\square$

Remark D.1 (Correction to the earlier version). The earlier version claimed that correlated decay "slows the mid-tail, accelerates the deep tail." This is incorrect: as Corollary 8.2 shows, the frailty model with positive $\theta$ has survival at least as large as the independent-baseline model at every $t$. The correct interpretation is that ignoring correlation underestimates the half-life, because the independent model with the same marginals assigns lower joint survival to the "all must survive" event. The frailty model corrects this by allowing failures to cluster.

Remark D.2 (Heavy tail). The frailty survival decays like $\Lambda^{-1/\theta}$ for large $\Lambda$, a polynomial decay rather than the exponential decay $e^{-\Lambda}$ of the independent-baseline model. The frailty model therefore has a heavier tail, and the $\varepsilon$-life $t_\varepsilon$ is much longer than the independent-baseline prediction for small $\varepsilon$.

D.4 Calibration against the empirical record

Protocol. The baseline survival functions $\bar F_{c,0}$ and the frailty parameter $\theta$ are estimated by maximum likelihood on a calibration set drawn from the reproducibility-decay literature (workflow-decay studies, package-registry rebuildability studies, and Software Heritage rebuildability traces). The parametric form of $\bar F_{c,0}$ is chosen from a pre-registered set (exponential, Weibull, log-normal) by AIC on the calibration set. The fitted model's half-life is compared to the empirical half-life of the calibration set, with a bootstrap confidence interval. A model whose half-life lies outside the empirical confidence interval is rejected before the confirmatory phase.

