Appendix E — Ablation calculus

E.1 Proof of Proposition 9

Proposition 9. For loss classes $c, c'$, the operators $A_c \circ A_{c'}$ and $A_{c'} \circ A_c$ agree on the loss state, are idempotent, and are monotone: $A_c \circ A_c = A_c$. For perturbation, $A_{\mathrm{pert}}$ is not idempotent in general and does not commute with loss operators.

Proof. For loss classes, $A_c(P,L) = (P, L \setminus \{c\})$, so $A_c \circ A_{c'}(P,L) = (P, L \setminus \{c, c'\}) = A_{c'} \circ A_c(P,L)$; idempotence is immediate from $(L \setminus \{c\}) \setminus \{c\} = L \setminus \{c\}$. For perturbation, $A_{\mathrm{pert}}(P,L) = (P', L)$ with $M' \neq M$; applying twice gives $M'' \neq M'$ in general, so $A_{\mathrm{pert}}^2 \neq A_{\mathrm{pert}}$; and $A_{\mathrm{pert}} \circ A_c$ changes $M$ while $A_c \circ A_{\mathrm{pert}}$ changes $L$ first, and the two do not commute because the perturbation is applied to the manifest while the loss is applied to the loss state. $\square$

Remark E.1. The loss states form a distributive lattice under $\subseteq$; the perturbation operator acts on the manifest only, not on the lattice. The empirical protocol reports loss-class results on the lattice and perturbation results separately.

