Appendix F — Bayes-optimal mapping and cost frontier

F.1 Proof of Proposition 10 (Bayes-optimal abstention)

Proposition 10. Fix a trial $(P,L)$ and let $p = \mathbb{P}(R = \mathsf{V} \mid O(P,L))$ be the posterior. Fix costs $c_{\mathsf{FV}}, c_{\mathsf{FN}}, c_{\mathsf{U}} > 0$. The Bayes-optimal rule is
\psi^\ast(o) = \begin{cases} \mathsf{V} & \text{if } (1-p)c_{\mathsf{FV}} \le \min\{p c_{\mathsf{FN}}, c_{\mathsf{U}}\}, \\ \mathsf{N} & \text{if } p c_{\mathsf{FN}} \le \min\{(1-p)c_{\mathsf{FV}}, c_{\mathsf{U}}\}, \\ \mathsf{U} & \text{otherwise}. \end{cases}

Proof. The expected cost of action $a$ given observation $o$ is $\sum_r \mathbb{P}(R=r \mid o)\,\mathrm{cost}(a,r)$. Enumerating: $\mathbb{E}[\mathrm{cost}(\mathsf{V} \mid o)] = (1-p)c_{\mathsf{FV}}$ (since $\mathrm{cost}(\mathsf{V},\mathsf{V})=0$ and $\mathrm{cost}(\mathsf{V},\mathsf{N})=c_{\mathsf{FV}}$); $\mathbb{E}[\mathrm{cost}(\mathsf{N} \mid o)] = p\,c_{\mathsf{FN}}$; $\mathbb{E}[\mathrm{cost}(\mathsf{U} \mid o)] = c_{\mathsf{U}}$. The Bayes action is the argmin. $\square$

F.2 Proof of Corollary 10 (Two-threshold form, corrected)

Corollary 10. Under the non-degeneracy condition $c_{\mathsf{U}} < c_{\mathsf{FV}} c_{\mathsf{FN}}/(c_{\mathsf{FV}} + c_{\mathsf{FN}})$, the Bayes rule is
\psi^\ast(o) = \begin{cases} \mathsf{N} & \text{if } p \le \tau_L, \\ \mathsf{U} & \text{if } \tau_L < p < \tau_H, \\ \mathsf{V} & \text{if } p \ge \tau_H, \end{cases}


with $\tau_L = c_{\mathsf{U}}/c_{\mathsf{FN}}$ and $\tau_H = 1 - c_{\mathsf{U}}/c_{\mathsf{FV}}$. If the non-degeneracy fails, the $\mathsf{U}$ region is empty and the rule is the binary Bayes classifier with threshold $c_{\mathsf{FV}}/(c_{\mathsf{FV}} + c_{\mathsf{FN}})$.

Proof. From the three expected costs:

· $\mathsf{V}$ optimal $\iff$ $p \ge c_{\mathsf{FV}}/(c_{\mathsf{FV}}+c_{\mathsf{FN}})$ and $p \ge 1 - c_{\mathsf{U}}/c_{\mathsf{FV}}$. Hence $\mathsf{V}$ optimal on $[\max\{c_{\mathsf{FV}}/(c_{\mathsf{FV}}+c_{\mathsf{FN}}), 1 - c_{\mathsf{U}}/c_{\mathsf{FV}}\}, 1]$.
· $\mathsf{N}$ optimal $\iff$ $p \le c_{\mathsf{FV}}/(c_{\mathsf{FV}}+c_{\mathsf{FN}})$ and $p \le c_{\mathsf{U}}/c_{\mathsf{FN}}$. Hence $\mathsf{N}$ optimal on $[0, \min\{c_{\mathsf{FV}}/(c_{\mathsf{FV}}+c_{\mathsf{FN}}), c_{\mathsf{U}}/c_{\mathsf{FN}}\}]$.
· $\mathsf{U}$ optimal $\iff$ $c_{\mathsf{U}} \le (1-p)c_{\mathsf{FV}}$ and $c_{\mathsf{U}} \le p c_{\mathsf{FN}}$, i.e., $p \le 1 - c_{\mathsf{U}}/c_{\mathsf{FV}}$ and $p \ge c_{\mathsf{U}}/c_{\mathsf{FN}}$. Hence $\mathsf{U}$ optimal on $[c_{\mathsf{U}}/c_{\mathsf{FN}}, 1 - c_{\mathsf{U}}/c_{\mathsf{FV}}]$.

Under non-degeneracy $c_{\mathsf{U}} < c_{\mathsf{FV}}c_{\mathsf{FN}}/(c_{\mathsf{FV}}+c_{\mathsf{FN}})$, both $c_{\mathsf{U}}/c_{\mathsf{FN}} < c_{\mathsf{FV}}/(c_{\mathsf{FV}}+c_{\mathsf{FN}})$ and $c_{\mathsf{FV}}/(c_{\mathsf{FV}}+c_{\mathsf{FN}}) < 1 - c_{\mathsf{U}}/c_{\mathsf{FV}}$ hold (each equivalent to the non-degeneracy). So $\tau_L = c_{\mathsf{U}}/c_{\mathsf{FN}}$, $\tau_H = 1 - c_{\mathsf{U}}/c_{\mathsf{FV}}$, and $\tau_L < \tau_H$. If non-degeneracy fails, both $\tau_L$ and $\tau_H$ collapse to $c_{\mathsf{FV}}/(c_{\mathsf{FV}}+c_{\mathsf{FN}})$. $\square$

Remark F.1 (Correction). The earlier version stated $\tau_L = c_{\mathsf{U}}/(c_{\mathsf{U}} + c_{\mathsf{FN}})$. The corrected threshold is $\tau_L = c_{\mathsf{U}}/c_{\mathsf{FN}}$. The corrected threshold enlarges the $\mathsf{N}$ region and shrinks the $\mathsf{U}$ region at the lower end. The correction is used throughout the revised protocol.

F.3 Proof of Proposition 11 (Monotone flip structure)

Proposition 11. Along any ray in cost space that fixes $c_{\mathsf{FV}}/c_{\mathsf{FN}}$ and $c_{\mathsf{U}}/c_{\mathsf{FN}}$ and scales the overall magnitude, the adoption decision is constant. Along a ray that fixes $c_{\mathsf{FV}}/c_{\mathsf{FN}}$ and varies $c_{\mathsf{U}}/c_{\mathsf{FN}}$, the adoption decision changes at most once, at the threshold where the $\mathsf{U}$ region collapses.

Proof. The mapping $\psi^\ast$ depends only on the ratios, not the absolute magnitudes. Along the $\mathsf{U}$-ratio ray, the $\mathsf{U}$ region is non-empty iff the non-degeneracy inequality $c_{\mathsf{U}}/c_{\mathsf{FN}} < (c_{\mathsf{FV}}/c_{\mathsf{FN}})/(1 + c_{\mathsf{FV}}/c_{\mathsf{FN}})$ holds, which is a single threshold in $c_{\mathsf{U}}/c_{\mathsf{FN}}$. Below the threshold, the rule is tri-state; above it, binary. Since the adoption decision is a function of the rule's outputs, it can change only at this threshold. $\square$

F.4 Proof of Proposition 16 (Flip-surface structure)

Proposition 16. Under the two-threshold rule, the flip surface in the $(c_{\mathsf{U}}/c_{\mathsf{FN}}, c_{\mathsf{U}}/c_{\mathsf{FV}})$ plane is a single monotone curve separating the region where $\mathsf{U}$ is non-empty from the region where it is empty.

Proof. The non-degeneracy condition is $c_{\mathsf{U}} < c_{\mathsf{FV}}c_{\mathsf{FN}}/(c_{\mathsf{FV}}+c_{\mathsf{FN}})$, equivalent to $1/(c_{\mathsf{U}}/c_{\mathsf{FV}}) + 1/(c_{\mathsf{U}}/c_{\mathsf{FN}}) > 1$. Setting $x = c_{\mathsf{U}}/c_{\mathsf{FV}}$ and $y = c_{\mathsf{U}}/c_{\mathsf{FN}}$, the condition is $1/x + 1/y > 1$, i.e., $x+y < xy$, a single monotone curve in the $(x,y)$ plane. $\square$

F.5 Worked numerical example

With $(c_{\mathsf{FV}}, c_{\mathsf{FN}}, c_{\mathsf{U}}) = (10, 2, 1)$: non-degeneracy check $c_{\mathsf{U}} = 1 < 20/12 \approx 1.667$ holds. Corrected thresholds: $\tau_L = 1/2 = 0.5$, $\tau_H = 1 - 1/10 = 0.9$. Rule: $\mathsf{N}$ if $p < 0.5$, $\mathsf{U}$ if $0.5 \le p \le 0.9$, $\mathsf{V}$ if $p > 0.9$. The earlier version's (incorrect) $\tau_L = 1/3$ would enlarge the $\mathsf{N}$ region and cause over-abstention on borderline trials with $p \in [1/3, 1/2]$.

