Appendix G — Statistical theory

G.1 Proof of Lemma 3 (Beta-binomial moments)

Lemma 3. Under $p_i \sim \mathrm{Beta}(\alpha, \beta)$ and $X_{ij} \mid p_i \sim \mathrm{Bernoulli}(p_i)$:
\mu = \mathbb{E}[X_{ij}] = \frac{\alpha}{\alpha+\beta}, \qquad \rho = \mathrm{Corr}(X_{ij}, X_{ik}) = \frac{1}{\alpha+\beta+1} \quad (j \neq k).

Proof. Law of total expectation: $\mathbb{E}[X_{ij}] = \mathbb{E}[p_i] = \alpha/(\alpha+\beta)$. Law of total variance: $\mathrm{Var}(X_{ij}) = \mathbb{E}[p_i(1-p_i)] + \mathrm{Var}(p_i) = \alpha\beta/((\alpha+\beta)(\alpha+\beta+1)) + \alpha\beta/((\alpha+\beta)^2(\alpha+\beta+1)) = \alpha\beta/((\alpha+\beta)^2) = \mu(1-\mu)$. Covariance: $\mathrm{Cov}(X_{ij}, X_{ik}) = \mathbb{E}[p_i^2] - \mu^2 = \mathrm{Var}(p_i) = \alpha\beta/((\alpha+\beta)^2(\alpha+\beta+1))$. Then $\rho = \mathrm{Cov}/\mathrm{Var} = 1/(\alpha+\beta+1)$. $\square$

G.2 Proof of Lemma 4 (Design effect)

Lemma 4. With $\bar X = (1/(Tm))\sum_{ij} X_{ij}$,
\mathrm{Var}(\bar X) = \frac{\mu(1-\mu)}{Tm}[1 + (m-1)\rho], \qquad n_{\mathrm{eff}}(\rho; T, m) = \frac{Tm}{1 + (m-1)\rho}.

Proof. Independence across templates gives zero covariance across templates. Within a template, covariances are $\rho\mu(1-\mu)$ for $j \neq j'$, and there are $Tm(m-1)$ such ordered pairs. So $\mathrm{Var}(\sum_{ij} X_{ij}) = Tm\mu(1-\mu) + Tm(m-1)\rho\mu(1-\mu) = Tm\mu(1-\mu)[1+(m-1)\rho]$. Divide by $(Tm)^2$. $\square$

G.3 Proof of Lemmas 5–6 (Clopper-Pearson zero-error bounds)

Lemma 5. With $X = 0$ in $n$ trials, the one-sided $95\%$ CP upper bound on $p$ is $U = 1 - 0.05^{1/n}$.

Proof. The CP upper bound is the $0.95$-quantile of $\mathrm{Beta}(X+1, n-X)$; at $X=0$ this is $\mathrm{Beta}(1,n)$, whose survival function is $(1-p)^n$. Setting $(1-U)^n = 0.05$ gives $U = 1 - 0.05^{1/n}$. $\square$

Lemma 6. With $n$ trials and zero abstentions, the one-sided $95\%$ CP lower bound on coverage is $L = 0.05^{1/(n+1)}$.

Proof. By symmetry, the CP lower bound at $X=n$ (no abstentions) is the $0.05$-quantile of $\mathrm{Beta}(n+1,1)$, whose CDF is $x^{n+1}$. The $0.05$-quantile is $0.05^{1/(n+1)}$. Requiring $L \ge 0.80$ gives $n+1 \ge \ln 0.05/\ln 0.80 \approx 13.4$, i.e., $n \ge 13$, consistent with $n=14$. $\square$

G.4 Proof of Proposition 12 (Design requirement)

Proposition 12. Under zero observed errors and plug-in $\rho_{\mathrm{upper}}$,
T_{\mathrm{req}}(\rho; m) = \left\lceil \frac{n_0[1+(m-1)\rho]}{m} \right\rceil, \qquad n_0 = \left\lceil \frac{\ln 0.05}{\ln 0.95} \right\rceil = 59.

Proof. From Lemma 5, $1 - 0.05^{1/n} \le 0.05$ iff $n \ge \ln 0.05/\ln 0.95 \approx 58.4$, so $n_0 = 59$. Substitute $n_{\mathrm{eff}} = Tm/[1+(m-1)\rho] \ge n_0$ and solve for $T$. $\square$

G.5 Proof of Proposition 13 (Max-$t$ validity)

Proposition 13. Under the bootstrap consistency conditions of Proposition 15, the max-$t$ interval $\max_c (U_c - q_{1-\alpha}\hat\sigma_c)$ covers $\max_c \mathrm{FV}_c$ with probability at least $1 - \alpha_{\mathrm{FWER}} + o(1)$.

Proof. The studentised maximum $T_n = \max_c (U_c - \mathrm{FV}_c)/\hat\sigma_c$ converges in distribution to $\max_c Z_c$ with $Z_c$ standard normal and correlated according to the class-level correlation. The bootstrap analogue $T_n^\ast$ converges to the same limit in probability (Donsker, Hadamard differentiability of the max functional). The quantile $q_{1-\alpha}$ of $\max_c Z_c$ is estimated by the bootstrap quantile of $T_n^\ast$; the resulting interval has asymptotic coverage $1 - \alpha_{\mathrm{FWER}}$. $\square$

G.6 Proof of Proposition 14 (Split-$\rho$ certification)

Proposition 14. Split the development set into halves $\mathcal{D}_1, \mathcal{D}_2$. Estimate $\hat\rho_1$ on $\mathcal{D}_1$ and certify on $\mathcal{D}_2$ using $n_{\mathrm{eff}}(\hat\rho_1)$; then swap and average. Under the beta-binomial model with $\hat\rho$ consistent, the split-$\rho$ certificate has coverage at least $1 - \alpha - o(1)$.

Proof. The split decouples the estimation error from the certification sample. Each half-certificate is a function of an independent estimation sample and an independent certification sample; by the cross-fitting argument, the average has coverage $1 - \alpha - o(1)$. $\square$

Remark G.1. The earlier version's single-$\rho$ certificate was anti-conservative whenever the development and certification samples overlapped in $\rho$-estimation. The split-$\rho$ certificate removes this anti-conservatism at the cost of a slightly wider bound.

G.7 Proof of Proposition 15 (Bootstrap consistency)

Proposition 15. Under (i) independence of templates, (ii) $\mathbb{E}[\hat p^2] < \infty$, and (iii) $\sqrt{T}(\hat p - \mu) \to \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2)$, the percentile cluster bootstrap upper bound $\hat U$ is consistent for $\mu + z_{0.95}\sigma/\sqrt{T}$.

Proof. Let $\mathbb{P}_T$ be the empirical measure of templates, $\mathbb{P}_T^\ast$ the bootstrap measure. By (iii), $\sqrt{T}(\mathbb{P}_T - \mu) \rightsquigarrow \mathbb{G}$ in $\ell^\infty(\mathbb{R})$. By Donsker's theorem for the multinomial bootstrap, $\sqrt{T}(\mathbb{P}_T^\ast - \mathbb{P}_T) \rightsquigarrow \mathbb{G}$ in probability conditional on the data. The quantile functional is Hadamard-differentiable at the limit quantile (van der Vaart 1998, §23.2), so the functional delta method gives $\hat U \xrightarrow{P} \mu + z_{0.95}\sigma/\sqrt{T}$. $\square$

Remark G.2 (All-zero fallback). At all-zero data, the percentile bootstrap returns $\hat U = 0$, which is anti-conservative. The implementation falls back to Lemma 5 with $n = n_{\mathrm{eff}}$, which is the conservative bound $1 - 0.05^{1/n_{\mathrm{eff}}}$. The fallback is pre-registered and does not affect the asymptotic consistency, because the probability of all-zero data tends to zero when $\mu > 0$.

G.8 Nonzero $\mu_{\mathrm{null}}$: exact computation

For $\mu_{\mathrm{null}} > 0$, the certification probability is computed by exact binomial-tail enumeration:
\mathbb{P}(U \le 0.05 \mid \mu = \mu_{\mathrm{null}}) = \sum_{k=0}^n \mathbb{P}(\mathrm{Bin}(n, \mu_{\mathrm{null}}) = k)\,\mathbf{1}\{\mathbb{P}(\mathrm{Bin}(n, 0.05) \le k) \le 0.05\}.


This is exact and computable; the normal approximation is inaccurate at small $n$ and small $\mu_{\mathrm{null}}$ because the CP bound is a highly skewed Beta quantile near zero. The design table in Appendix I is computed by exact enumeration.

