Appendix H — End-to-end soundness with fallible reference

H.1 Proof of Theorem 1

Theorem 1. Assume Algorithm 1 is executed as specified, $\tau$ and $\psi$ are pre-registered and unmodified between Phase B and Phase D, the split-$\rho$ certificate is used, and the reference error $\epsilon_{\mathrm{ref}}$ is estimated as in Proposition 3 with upper bound $\bar\epsilon_{\mathrm{ref}}$. Then for each candidate $f$ and each cost triple $c \in \mathcal{G}$, with probability at least $1 - \eta_{\mathrm{split}} - 0.05/K$ (over split estimation and trial sample),
\hat U_f(c) \ge \mathrm{FV}^\star(f \mid c),


where $\mathrm{FV}^\star$ is the true (unobservable) false-VERIFIABLE rate and $\hat U_f(c)$ is the reported bound after propagation.

Proof. Let $E_{\mathrm{split}}$ be the event that the split-$\rho$ certificate covers $\mathrm{FV}(f \mid c)$ at level $1 - 0.05/K$ (Bonferroni over $K$ candidates). By Proposition 14, $\mathbb{P}(E_{\mathrm{split}}) \ge 1 - \eta_{\mathrm{split}} - o(1)$.

Conditional on $E_{\mathrm{split}}$, the measured false-VERIFIABLE rate $\mathrm{FV}(f \mid c)$ is bounded above by the split-$\rho$ upper bound $U_f^{\mathrm{split}}(c)$. By Corollary B.1, the true rate satisfies $\mathrm{FV}^\star(f \mid c) \le \mathrm{FV}(f \mid c)(1 - \bar\epsilon_{\mathsf{N}}) + \bar\epsilon_{\mathsf{N}} \le U_f^{\mathrm{split}}(c)(1 - \bar\epsilon_{\mathsf{N}}) + \bar\epsilon_{\mathsf{N}} =: \hat U_f(c)$.

Combining the two events,
\mathbb{P}(\hat U_f(c) \ge \mathrm{FV}^\star(f \mid c)) \ge \mathbb{P}(E_{\mathrm{split}}) \ge 1 - \eta_{\mathrm{split}} - 0.05/K. \qquad \square

Remark H.1. The bound is conservative in three directions: (i) the split-$\rho$ slack $\eta_{\mathrm{split}}$, (ii) the CP bound's nominal $0.05/K$, (iii) the propagation inflation $\bar\epsilon_{\mathsf{N}}$. The first is typically small (a few hundred templates suffice for a tight $\rho$ bound); the third is the dominant slack when the reference is noisy. The protocol reports all three and their contributions to the total bound.

H.2 Worst-class propagation

For the co-primary worst-class bound, Theorem 1 is applied per class with the max-$t$ correction of Proposition 13. The per-class bound is
\hat U_f^{(c)} = U_f^{\mathrm{split},(c)}(1 - \bar\epsilon_{\mathsf{N}}^{(c)}) + \bar\epsilon_{\mathsf{N}}^{(c)},


and the reported worst-class bound is $\max_c \hat U_f^{(c)}$ with the max-$t$ critical value.

