Appendix J — Worked examples

J.1 Integrity-only verifier at dependency loss

Take the Minimal Integrity Profile with native outputs INTEGRITY_MATCH, INTEGRITY_MISMATCH, INVALID_RECORD, mapped to $\mathsf{V}, \mathsf{N}, \mathsf{U}$ respectively. For a package with intact bytes but a removed numpy==1.26.0 dependency, $R(P,L) = \mathsf{N}$ (re-execution fails at import). The verifier computes $O_{\mathrm{byte}}(P,L) = \mathrm{SHA256}(B)$, which equals $O_{\mathrm{byte}}(P,C(P))$, so it outputs INTEGRITY_MATCH and $f(P,L) = \mathsf{V}$. By Corollary 4, $\mathrm{FV}(f) = 1$ on the answered dependency-loss trials. The integrity-only pair is reported separately as an integrity-only ceiling and is never a candidate for adoption.

J.2 RO-Crate mapping at declaration perturbation

An RO-Crate 1.3 package with a requirements.txt pin numpy==1.26.0, perturbed to numpy==1.25.0. The roc-validator produces two RECOMMENDED-severity issues. Pre-registered mapping: RECOMMENDED-only → $\mathsf{U}$. The verifier abstains. If the reference is $\mathsf{N}$ (the new pin fails), the abstention is correct; if $\mathsf{V}$ (the pin change is harmless), the abstention costs a manual check. Under the alternative mapping (RECOMMENDED → $\mathsf{V}$), the verifier is unsound if the reference is $\mathsf{N}$. The band between the two mappings is the coverage–risk frontier.

J.3 EQR at partial loss

An EQR with eqr-core-1 profile listing five requirement ids. Under an ablation that removes the base image, base_image_available fails; the precedence rule maps the status to FAIL → $\mathsf{N}$. Reference is $\mathsf{N}$ (image unavailable). Correct. If instead the ablation removes a non-required data file, the status is INDETERMINATE → $\mathsf{U}$. If the reference is $\mathsf{V}$ (the optional data was unused), the abstention costs a manual check; if $\mathsf{N}$ (the data was silently required), the abstention is correct.

J.4 The naive control at three ablations

· Intact: hash match, link resolves. $\mathsf{V}$; reference $\mathsf{V}$; correct.
· Data removed: hash match, link 404. $\mathsf{N}$; reference $\mathsf{N}$; correct.
· Package removed: hash match, but link check does not resolve packages. $\mathsf{V}$; reference $\mathsf{N}$; incorrect.

The third case triggers the H2 dependency-class clause. If the naive control passes the pooled bound but fails on package and environment classes, H2 is not met and the naive control is not adopted.

J.5 Reference-error propagation with a noisy reference

Suppose the three-run protocol has $\bar\epsilon_{\mathsf{N}} = 0.02$ (2% of true re-executable packages are labelled $\mathsf{N}$ by the reference) and $\bar\epsilon_{\mathsf{V}} = 0.005$. A verifier measures $\mathrm{FV}(f) = 0.03$ on the held-out set and is $R$-complete. The sharp corrected bound is $\mathrm{FV}^\star(f) \le 0.03 \cdot 0.98 + 0.02 = 0.0494$, which exceeds the $0.05$ threshold marginally. The looser earlier-version bound is $(0.03 + 0.02)/(1 - 0.005 - 0.02) = 0.0513$. The protocol reports both; the sharp bound is the primary certificate, and the looser bound is a conservative cross-check. The candidate is INCONCLUSIVE on the sharp bound (0.0494 ≤ 0.05 but with the Bonferroni correction over candidates the effective threshold is $0.05/K$, so it fails).

J.6 Cost-matrix flip at $c_{\mathsf{U}} = 1$, $c_{\mathsf{FV}} = 10$, $c_{\mathsf{FN}} = 2$

Non-degeneracy: $c_{\mathsf{U}} = 1 < 20/12 \approx 1.667$ holds. Corrected thresholds: $\tau_L = 0.5$, $\tau_H = 0.9$. A verifier with measured $p$-values concentrated in $[0.4, 0.6]$ has $\mathsf{N}$ and $\mathsf{U}$ mixed. Under the earlier version's $\tau_L = 1/3$, the same $p$-values would be classified as $\mathsf{U}$ more often, increasing coverage at the cost of more false-VERIFIABLE. The corrected threshold reduces coverage but also reduces the false-VERIFIABLE rate; the net effect on the adoption decision depends on the candidate and is reported per candidate in the cost-frontier analysis.

