Appendix K — Reference implementation and reproducibility

K.1 Containerised pipeline

The complete pipeline is released as a container image with a replay script, so that the empirical instantiation of the theory is itself verifiable. The image includes:

· Runtime: a pinned OCI image digest, with unshare -rn network namespace support.
· Mirror: a filesystem-mounted package mirror, with no loopback proxying.
· BLAS/LAPACK: a pinned OpenBLAS version, with thread-pinning environment variables per Algorithm 4.
· Reference oracle: Algorithm 1 implemented in Python 3.11, with strace postcondition enforcement per Lemma 7.
· Statistical analysis: the CP bounds, max-$t$ correction, split-$\rho$ certificate, and bootstrap in a pinned scipy version.
· Power simulation: the exact binomial-tail enumeration and the $T_{\text{req}}$ table generation.
· Replay script: a single command that regenerates all tables and figures from the sealed records.

K.2 Artifact identifiers

The protocol, schemas, mapping, tolerance record, $\rho_{\text{split}}$, $\bar\epsilon_{\mathrm{ref}}$, $T_{\text{test}}$, development records, and sealed test labels are hashed and archived with a DOI and a Software Heritage swhid. The analysis code and the power-simulation script are committed and hashed before Phase C. The replay script's output is compared byte-for-byte to the reported tables; any discrepancy is a reproducibility failure of the pipeline itself.

K.3 Known limitations

The pipeline is not a proof that the theory's predictions hold on arbitrary artifacts. It is a pre-registered instantiation on a specific held-out set. The theory's falsifiable predictions (main text §12) are tested against this instantiation; a failed prediction is a result, not a bug, and is reported as such. The container image is a fixed point of the pipeline's own reproducibility claim: it is verifiable in the sense the theory demands (byte digest, declared dependencies, isolated re-execution), but its own half-life is subject to the same decay process the theory describes. The protocol reports the pipeline's own $t_{1/2}$ as a self-referential quantity.

